// Lessons. Markup: {Latin = English} example; [[N:nom]] noun table; [[A:lemma]] adjective; [[V:verb|mood|voice|tenses]]; [[P:name]] pronoun; [[I:verb]] irregular; [[NF:verb]] non-finite forms
// dr = drill preset: {k:'noun',d:[..]} | {k:'verb',t:[...]} | {k:'adj'} | {k:'parse'}
const UNITS=['Foundations','Nouns & the present system','The perfect system, pronouns & the passive','Verbal nouns & adjectives','The subjunctive','The syntax of the cases','Advanced reading skills'];
const LESSONS=[
{u:0,t:'Alphabet & pronunciation',b:`<p>Latin uses our alphabet minus <b>j</b> and <b>w</b> (and <b>u/v</b> were one letter). We teach <b>Restored Classical</b> pronunciation – how Cicero spoke.</p>
<h4>Vowels</h4><p>Every vowel is short or long. Long vowels carry a <b>macron</b> (ā ē ī ō ū). Length changes meaning: <span class=la>malum</span> "evil" vs <span class=la>mālum</span> "apple"; <span class=la>puella</span> (nom.) vs <span class=la>puellā</span> (abl.).</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th></tr><tr><td>a</td><td>"a" in <i>idea</i></td><td>"a" in <i>father</i></td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td><i>pet</i></td><td><i>they</i></td></tr><tr><td>i</td><td><i>pit</i></td><td><i>machine</i></td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td><i>off</i></td><td><i>hope</i></td></tr><tr><td>u</td><td><i>put</i></td><td><i>rule</i></td></tr></table>
<h4>Diphthongs</h4><p><b>ae</b> = "eye" · <b>au</b> = "ow" · <b>oe</b> = "oy" · <b>ei</b> = "ay" · <b>eu</b> = "eh-oo" · <b>ui</b> = "oo-ee".</p>
<h4>Consonants</h4><ul><li><b>c</b> and <b>g</b> always hard: <span class=la>Cicerō</span> = "Kikero".</li><li><b>v</b> = English w: <span class=la>vīnum</span> = "weenum".</li><li><b>i</b> before a vowel at the start of a word = y: <span class=la>iam</span> = "yam".</li><li><b>s</b> always as in <i>sit</i>; <b>r</b> rolled; <b>th, ph, ch</b> = t, p, k with a puff of air.</li><li>Double consonants are held: <span class=la>an-nus</span>.</li></ul>
<h4>Stress</h4><p>Words of two syllables stress the first. Longer words stress the <b>second-to-last</b> syllable (the penult) if it is long – i.e. has a long vowel or ends in a consonant – otherwise the third-to-last. <span class=la>a-MĪ-cus</span>, <span class=la>FĒ-mi-na</span>, <span class=la>pu-EL-la</span>.</p>`,
q:[['How is "Cicerō" pronounced in Classical Latin?',['Sis-er-oh','Kee-keh-roh','Chee-cheh-roh'],1,'C is always hard.'],['What does a macron mark?',['Stress','A long vowel','A silent letter'],1,'Macrons mark long vowels, which can change meaning.'],['Where is the stress in "fēmina"?',['fē-','-mi-','-na'],0,'Penult "mi" is short, so stress falls on the antepenult.']]},
{u:0,t:'How Latin works: inflection',b:`<p>English shows who does what to whom mostly by <b>word order</b>: "The dog bites the man" ≠ "The man bites the dog". Latin shows it with <b>endings</b>. Word order is free and used for emphasis.</p>
{Canis virum mordet. = The dog bites the man.}{Virum canis mordet. = The dog bites the man (emphasis on "man").}{Canem vir mordet. = The man bites the dog.}
<h4>Key terms</h4><ul><li><b>Inflection</b>: changing a word's ending to show its job.</li><li><b>Declension</b>: the set of endings for nouns/adjectives (5 declensions).</li><li><b>Conjugation</b>: the set of endings for verbs (4 conjugations).</li><li><b>Stem</b>: the part that stays the same; <b>ending</b>: the part that changes.</li></ul>
<h4>Nouns have</h4><p><b>Gender</b> (masculine, feminine, neuter), <b>number</b> (singular, plural), <b>case</b> (job in the sentence).</p>
<h4>Verbs have</h4><p><b>Person</b> (I/you/he; we/you/they), <b>number</b>, <b>tense</b> (6), <b>mood</b> (indicative, subjunctive, imperative), <b>voice</b> (active, passive). The ending alone tells you the subject: <span class=la>amō</span> = I love, <span class=la>amant</span> = they love. Subject pronouns are usually left out.</p>
<h4>Normal word order</h4><p>Subject – Object – Verb is the default in prose: {Puella rosam amat. = The girl loves the rose.} Adjectives usually follow their nouns; there are no words for "a" or "the".</p>`,
q:[['What tells you the subject of "amant"?',['Word order','The ending -nt','A pronoun'],1,'-nt = "they".'],['Does Latin have a word for "the"?',['Yes','No'],1,'Latin has no articles; supply "a" or "the" as English needs.'],['Default prose word order?',['Subject–Verb–Object','Subject–Object–Verb','Verb–Subject–Object'],1,'The verb usually comes last.']]},
{u:0,t:'The six cases',b:`<p>Each case marks a job. Learn these core uses now; Unit 6 covers every detail.</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>Case</th><th>Main job</th><th>English clue</th></tr>
<tr><td>Nominative</td><td>Subject; complement after "be"</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Genitive</td><td>Possession, "of"</td><td>of, 's</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dative</td><td>Indirect object</td><td>to, for</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accusative</td><td>Direct object; motion toward; with many prepositions</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ablative</td><td>Means, manner, place where, time when, separation; with many prepositions</td><td>by, with, from, in, on, at</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vocative</td><td>Direct address</td><td>O …!</td></tr></table>
{Fīlia agricolae nautae aquam dat. = The farmer's daughter gives water to the sailor.}
<p><span class=la>fīlia</span> nom. (subject) · <span class=la>agricolae</span> gen. (of the farmer) · <span class=la>nautae</span> dat. (to the sailor) · <span class=la>aquam</span> acc. (object).</p>
<p><b>Tip:</b> Also learn the <b>locative</b> – a leftover case for names of cities and a few words: <span class=la>Rōmae</span> "at Rome", <span class=la>domī</span> "at home".</p>`,
q:[['Which case marks the direct object?',['Nominative','Dative','Accusative'],2,''],['"to/for" usually = ?',['Genitive','Dative','Ablative'],1,''],['Which case is used for address ("O Marcus!")?',['Vocative','Nominative','Genitive'],0,'']]},
{u:1,t:'First declension',dr:{k:'noun',d:[1]},b:`<p>First-declension nouns end in <b>-a</b> (nom.) and <b>-ae</b> (gen.). Most are feminine; a few that denote men's jobs are masculine (<span class=la>nauta, agricola, poēta</span>).</p>
<p><b>Always learn nouns as: nominative, genitive, gender.</b> The genitive shows the declension and gives the stem (drop <b>-ae</b>).</p>[[N:puella]]
<p>Notice the overlaps: <span class=la>puellae</span> can be gen. sg., dat. sg., or nom. pl.; <span class=la>puellīs</span> dat. or abl. pl. Context and the rest of the sentence decide.</p>
{Poēta puellīs fābulam nārrat. = The poet tells the girls a story.}{Fēminae aquam portant. = The women carry water.}`,
q:[['Case(s) of "puellam"?',['Acc. sg.','Gen. sg.','Abl. sg.'],0,''],['"puellārum" means…',['of the girls','to the girls','the girls (obj.)'],0,'Genitive plural.'],['Gender of "nauta"?',['Feminine','Masculine'],1,'Men\'s occupations are masculine even in the 1st declension.']]},
{u:1,t:'Present tense: 1st & 2nd conjugations, and sum',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.pres.A'],c:[1,2]},b:`<p>Verbs are learned by their four <b>principal parts</b>: <span class=la>amō, amāre, amāvī, amātum</span>. The 2nd part (infinitive) shows the conjugation: <b>-āre</b> = 1st, <b>-ēre</b> = 2nd, <b>-ere</b> = 3rd, <b>-īre</b> = 4th.</p>
<h4>Personal endings (active)</h4><table class=t><tr><th></th><th>Sg</th><th>Pl</th></tr><tr><td>1st</td><td>-ō / -m (I)</td><td>-mus (we)</td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td>-s (you)</td><td>-tis (you all)</td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td>-t (he/she/it)</td><td>-nt (they)</td></tr></table>
<p>Present stem = infinitive minus <b>-re</b>: <span class=la>amā-</span>, <span class=la>monē-</span>. Add the endings (the ā shortens before -t, -nt, and disappears in amō).</p>
[[V:amō|ind|A|pres]][[V:moneō|ind|A|pres]]
<p>The present means "I love", "I am loving", "I do love".</p><h4>The verb "to be"</h4>[[I:sum]]
{Agricola terram amat. = The farmer loves the land.}{Puellae nōn timent. = The girls are not afraid.}{Poēta sum. = I am a poet.}`,
q:[['"vocant" means…',['they call','we call','he calls'],0,''],['Conjugation of "habeō, habēre"?',['1st','2nd','3rd'],1,'-ēre = 2nd.'],['"estis" =',['you (pl.) are','they are','we are'],0,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Second declension: masculine',dr:{k:'noun',d:[2]},b:`<p>Genitive in <b>-ī</b>. Most nouns in <b>-us</b> are masculine. Some end in <b>-er</b> (<span class=la>puer, puerī</span>; <span class=la>ager, agrī</span> – the genitive shows whether the e stays) and one in <b>-ir</b> (<span class=la>vir, virī</span>).</p>[[N:amīcus]][[N:puer]][[N:ager]]
<p><b>Vocative:</b> nouns in <b>-us</b> have vocative <b>-e</b> (<span class=la>amīce!</span>); those in <b>-ius</b> have <b>-ī</b> (<span class=la>fīlī!</span>). Everywhere else in Latin, vocative = nominative.</p>
{Servī dominō cibum dant. = The slaves give food to the master.}{Et tū, Brūte? = You too, Brutus?}`,
q:[['Vocative of "amīcus"?',['amīcus','amīce','amīcī'],1,''],['"agrōs" is…',['acc. pl.','gen. sg.','abl. pl.'],0,''],['Stem of "ager, agrī"?',['ager-','agr-'],1,'Take the stem from the genitive.']]},
{u:1,t:'Second declension neuter; adjective agreement',dr:{k:'adj'},b:`<p>Neuter nouns end in <b>-um</b>, gen. <b>-ī</b>.</p>[[N:bellum]]
<p><b>Two neuter rules that hold in every declension:</b> (1) nominative = accusative (= vocative); (2) nom./acc. plural end in <b>-a</b>.</p>
<h4>1st/2nd-declension adjectives</h4><p>Learned as <span class=la>bonus, bona, bonum</span>: masculine like <span class=la>amīcus</span>, feminine like <span class=la>puella</span>, neuter like <span class=la>bellum</span>.</p>[[A:bonus]]
<p><b>Agreement:</b> an adjective matches its noun in <b>gender, number and case</b> – not necessarily in ending: {agricola bonus = the good farmer (masc., so -us even with a 1st-decl. noun)}{Magnum dōnum puellae bonae dat. = He gives a great gift to the good girl.}
<p>Adjectives can stand alone as nouns: <span class=la>bonī</span> "good men", <span class=la>multa</span> "many things".</p>`,
q:[['Nom. pl. of "dōnum"?',['dōnī','dōna','dōnōs'],1,''],['Which is correct for "good sailor"?',['nauta bona','nauta bonus'],1,'nauta is masculine.'],['"magna" can agree with…',['fem. nom. sg. or neut. nom./acc. pl.','only fem. sg.'],0,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Prepositions',b:`<p>Prepositions take either the <b>accusative</b> or the <b>ablative</b>. A general principle: <b>accusative = motion toward / through</b>, <b>ablative = place where, motion away</b>.</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>+ Accusative</th><th>+ Ablative</th></tr><tr><td>ad to, toward<br>in into, onto, against<br>per through<br>trāns across<br>post after, behind<br>ante before<br>circum around<br>inter between, among<br>propter, ob because of<br>contrā against<br>apud among, at the house of</td><td>ā, ab from, by (agent)<br>ē, ex out of<br>dē down from, about<br>in in, on<br>cum with<br>sine without<br>prō in front of, on behalf of<br>sub under</td></tr></table>
<p><b>in</b> and <b>sub</b> take both: {in silvam = into the forest}{in silvā = in the forest}
<p>Use <b>ā/ē</b> before consonants, <b>ab/ex</b> before vowels or h. <b>cum</b> is attached to personal pronouns: <span class=la>mēcum</span>, <span class=la>tēcum</span>, <span class=la>nōbīscum</span>.</p>
{Cum amīcīs ex oppidō ad silvam ambulāmus. = With friends we walk out of the town to the forest.}`,
q:[['"in agrum" means…',['in the field','into the field'],1,'Accusative = motion.'],['Case after "sine"?',['Accusative','Ablative'],1,''],['"with me" =',['cum mē','mēcum'],1,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Imperfect & future (1st, 2nd; sum)',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.impf.A','ind.fut.A'],c:[1,2]},b:`<h4>Imperfect</h4><p>Ongoing or repeated past action: "was loving, used to love, kept loving". Sign: <b>-ba-</b>. Stem + <b>-bam, -bās, -bat, -bāmus, -bātis, -bant</b>.</p>
<h4>Future (1st & 2nd conjugation)</h4><p>Sign: <b>-bi-</b>. Stem + <b>-bō, -bis, -bit, -bimus, -bitis, -bunt</b>. (The 3rd and 4th conjugations form the future differently – lesson 11.)</p>
[[V:amō|ind|A|pres,impf,fut]]
<p>Sum: imperfect <span class=la>eram…</span>, future <span class=la>erō…</span> (see lesson 5 table).</p>
{Puerī in agrō labōrābant. = The boys were working in the field.}{Crās nāvigābimus. = Tomorrow we will sail.}{Ōlim poēta clārus erat. = Once he was a famous poet.}`,
q:[['"monēbunt" =',['they were warning','they will warn'],1,'-bunt = future.'],['Sign of the imperfect?',['-bi-','-ba-'],1,''],['"erit" =',['he was','he will be'],1,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Third declension',dr:{k:'noun',d:[3]},b:`<p>The biggest declension. Genitive in <b>-is</b>. The nominative is unpredictable, so <b>the genitive is essential</b> to find the stem: <span class=la>rēx, rēgis</span> → stem <span class=la>rēg-</span>; <span class=la>corpus, corporis</span> → <span class=la>corpor-</span>. Any gender is possible.</p>[[N:rēx]][[N:corpus]]
<h4>Gender tips</h4><ul><li>-or, -ōs, -er, -ēs (gen. -itis) → usually masculine</li><li>-iō, -tās, -tūs, -x, -is → usually feminine</li><li>-men, -us, -e, -al, -ar, -c, -t → usually neuter</li></ul>
<p>Remember: neuter nom. = acc., plural -a.</p>
{Rēx lēgēs cīvitātī dat. = The king gives laws to the state.}{Mīlitēs ducem laudant. = The soldiers praise the leader.}`,
q:[['Stem of "homō, hominis"?',['hom-','homin-'],1,''],['"corpora" is…',['nom./acc. pl.','abl. sg.'],0,'Neuter plural.'],['"rēgī" is…',['dat. sg.','gen. sg.','nom. pl.'],0,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Third, third -iō and fourth conjugations (present system)',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.pres.A','ind.impf.A','ind.fut.A'],c:[3,'3io',4]},b:`<p>The 3rd conjugation has a short <b>-e-</b> in the infinitive (<span class=la>regere</span>); its stem vowel varies (i, u, e). The 3rd -iō type (<span class=la>capiō, capere</span>) has an <b>i</b> in some forms; 4th (<span class=la>audiō, audīre</span>) has long <b>ī</b>.</p>
[[V:regō|ind|A|pres,impf,fut]][[V:capiō|ind|A|pres,impf,fut]][[V:audiō|ind|A|pres,impf,fut]]
<p><b>Future warning:</b> 3rd, 3rd -iō and 4th use <b>-am, -ēs, -et…</b> – not -bō. So <span class=la>reget</span> = "he will rule", but <span class=la>monet</span> (2nd) = "he warns". Remember: 1st & 2nd use <b>-bō, -bis, -bit</b>; 3rd & 4th use <b>-am, -ēs, -et</b>.</p>
<p>How to tell <span class=la>capiō</span> type from <span class=la>regō</span>: 1st part ends <b>-iō</b> but infinitive is <b>-ere</b>.</p>
{Mīlitēs urbem capient. = The soldiers will capture the city.}{Vōcem ducis audiēbāmus. = We were hearing the leader's voice.}`,
q:[['"dūcet" =',['he leads','he will lead','he was leading'],1,'3rd conj.: -et = future.'],['Which is a 3rd -iō verb?',['audiō, audīre','faciō, facere','moneō, monēre'],1,''],['"audiunt" =',['they hear','they will hear'],0,'']]},
{u:1,t:'Third-declension i-stems & third-declension adjectives',dr:{k:'noun',d:[3]},b:`<p>Some 3rd-declension nouns are <b>i-stems</b>, showing <b>-ium</b> in the genitive plural (neuters also <b>-ī</b> abl. sg. and <b>-ia</b> nom./acc. pl.).</p>
<h4>Which nouns are i-stems?</h4><ul><li><b>Parisyllabic</b>: same number of syllables in nom. and gen. (<span class=la>cīvis, cīvis; nāvis, nāvis</span>)</li><li>One syllable nom. ending in <b>-s/-x</b> with stem ending in two consonants (<span class=la>urbs, urbis; nox, noctis</span>)</li><li>Neuters in <b>-e, -al, -ar</b> (<span class=la>mare, animal</span>)</li></ul>[[N:cīvis]][[N:mare]]
<h4>3rd-declension adjectives</h4><p>Almost all decline as i-stems: abl. sg. <b>-ī</b>, gen. pl. <b>-ium</b>, neut. pl. <b>-ia</b>. They come in three types: three endings (<span class=la>ācer, ācris, ācre</span>), two endings (<span class=la>omnis, omne</span>), one ending (<span class=la>ingēns, gen. ingentis</span>).</p>[[A:omnis]][[A:ingēns]]
{Omnēs cīvēs urbem amant. = All the citizens love the city.}{In marī ingentī nāvigābant. = They were sailing on the huge sea.}`,
q:[['Gen. pl. of "urbs"?',['urbum','urbium'],1,'i-stem.'],['Abl. sg. of "omnis"?',['omne','omnī'],1,'3rd-decl. adjectives: -ī.'],['Nom. pl. of "mare"?',['mara','maria'],1,'Neuter i-stem.']]},
{u:1,t:'Fourth & fifth declensions',dr:{k:'noun',d:[4,5]},b:`<p><b>4th:</b> gen. <b>-ūs</b>; mostly masculine (-us), a few neuter (-ū). Watch for <b>-us</b> nouns that aren't 2nd declension – the genitive tells you.</p>[[N:manus]][[N:cornū]]
<p><b>5th:</b> gen. <b>-eī / -ēī</b>; mostly feminine (but <span class=la>diēs</span> usually masculine). Only <span class=la>rēs</span> and <span class=la>diēs</span> are common in the plural.</p>[[N:rēs]][[N:diēs]]
<p><b>rēs</b> is very flexible: thing, matter, affair, event, situation. <span class=la>rēs pūblica</span> = the state; <span class=la>rēs gestae</span> = deeds; <span class=la>rēs adversae</span> = misfortune.</p>
<p><b>domus</b> mixes 2nd and 4th: abl. <span class=la>domō</span>, acc. pl. <span class=la>domōs</span>, locative <span class=la>domī</span> "at home".</p>
{Senātus exercitum mittit. = The senate sends the army.}{Spem in manibus deōrum pōnimus. = We place our hope in the hands of the gods.}`,
q:[['Declension of "exercitus, exercitūs"?',['2nd','4th'],1,''],['"rērum" means…',['of things','things (obj.)'],0,''],['Locative of domus?',['domō','domī'],1,'']]},
{u:2,t:'The perfect system (active)',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.perf.A','ind.plup.A','ind.futp.A']},b:`<p>The 3rd principal part gives the <b>perfect stem</b> (drop -ī): <span class=la>amāvī → amāv-</span>, <span class=la>dūxī → dūx-</span>. The same endings apply to <b>every</b> verb, even irregular ones.</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>Perfect</th><th>Pluperfect</th><th>Future perfect</th></tr><tr><td>-ī, -istī, -it,<br>-imus, -istis, -ērunt</td><td>-eram, -erās, -erat,<br>-erāmus, -erātis, -erant</td><td>-erō, -eris, -erit,<br>-erimus, -eritis, -erint</td></tr></table>[[V:dūcō|ind|A|perf,plup,futp]]
<ul><li><b>Perfect</b>: completed past ("I led", "I have led").</li><li><b>Pluperfect</b>: before another past point ("I had led").</li><li><b>Future perfect</b>: completed before a future point ("I will have led"); often English uses a present: {Sī vēneris, gaudēbō. = If you come (will have come), I'll be glad.}</li></ul>
<h4>Common perfect patterns</h4><p>-v-/-u- (<span class=la>amāvī, monuī</span>); -s-/-x- (<span class=la>mīsī, rēxī</span>); lengthened vowel (<span class=la>vēnī, vīdī</span>); reduplication (<span class=la>dedī, cucurrī</span>); no change (<span class=la>dēfendī</span>). You must memorise the principal parts – the Words tab drills them.</p>`,
q:[['"vēnerant" =',['they had come','they will have come','they came'],0,''],['Perfect stem of "mittō, mittere, mīsī, missum"?',['mitt-','mīs-','miss-'],1,''],['"vīcistī" =',['you conquered','he conquered'],0,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Personal & reflexive pronouns, possessives',b:`[[P:ego / tū]]<p>Nominative pronouns are only used for emphasis or contrast: {Ego labōrō, tū dormīs. = I am working, you are sleeping.}
<h4>Reflexive</h4><p>Refers back to the subject. 1st/2nd person use the normal pronouns (<span class=la>mē laudō</span> "I praise myself"). 3rd person uses <b>suī</b>:</p>[[P:suī (reflexive)]]
{Sē laudat. = He praises himself.}{Eum laudat. = He praises him (someone else).}
<h4>Possessive adjectives</h4><p><span class=la>meus, tuus, noster, vester</span> and reflexive <span class=la>suus</span> ("his/her/their own") decline like bonus. For "his/her/their" <i>not</i> referring to the subject, use the genitive <span class=la>eius</span> / <span class=la>eōrum</span>:</p>
{Mārcus librum suum legit. = Marcus reads his (own) book.}{Mārcus librum eius legit. = Marcus reads his (someone else's) book.}<p><span class=la>nostrum/vestrum</span> are partitive ("one of us"), <span class=la>nostrī/vestrī</span> objective ("love of us").</p>`,
q:[['"sibi" is…',['dat. reflexive','acc. reflexive'],0,''],['"Iūlia mātrem suam amat" – whose mother?',['Julia\'s own','Someone else\'s'],0,''],['"with us" =',['cum nōs','nōbīscum'],1,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Demonstratives: is, hic, ille, ipse, īdem',b:`<p>Many pronouns have <b>-īus</b> gen. sg. and <b>-ī</b> dat. sg. in all genders.</p>[[P:is, ea, id]]<p><b>is</b> is the weak "he/she/it/they" or "this/that".</p>[[P:hic, haec, hoc]][[P:ille, illa, illud]]
<p><b>hic</b> = "this (here, near me)"; <b>ille</b> = "that (there)", also "the famous". Paired, <span class=la>hic … ille</span> = "the latter … the former".</p>[[P:ipse, ipsa, ipsum]]
<p><b>ipse</b> is intensive ("the man himself"), never reflexive: {Caesar ipse vēnit. = Caesar himself came.}</p>[[P:īdem, eadem, idem]]
<p><b>īdem</b> = is + -dem ("the same"); m → n before d (eundem). Also learn <b>iste, ista, istud</b> "that (of yours)" – often contemptuous – declined like ille.</p>`,
q:[['"huius" is…',['gen. sg. of hic','nom. pl. of hic'],0,''],['"the king himself" =',['rēx sē','rēx ipse'],1,''],['"eōdem diē" =',['on that day','on the same day'],1,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Relative pronoun & relative clauses',b:`[[P:quī, quae, quod]]<p><b>The rule:</b> the relative pronoun takes its <b>gender and number</b> from its antecedent, but its <b>case</b> from its job in its own clause.</p>
{Puella quam vidēs mea soror est. = The girl whom you see is my sister. (fem. sg. from puella; acc. because object of vidēs)}{Vir cui librum dedī poēta est. = The man to whom I gave the book is a poet.}{Urbs in quā habitāmus magna est. = The city in which we live is big.}
<p><b>Connecting relative:</b> at the start of a sentence, quī = "and he / this": {Quae cum audīvisset… = When he had heard these things…}</p>
<p>If there's no antecedent, supply one: {Quī tacet cōnsentit. = (He) who is silent agrees.}</p>`,
q:[['In "fēminae quās vīdī", why "quās"?',['fem. pl. from fēminae; acc. as object of vīdī','it agrees in case with fēminae'],0,''],['"cuius" =',['whose','whom'],0,''],['"quibuscum" =',['with whom (pl.)','to whom'],0,'cum attaches to quō/quā/quibus.']]},
{u:2,t:'Questions & interrogatives',b:`[[P:quis, quid (interrogative)]]<p><b>quis/quid</b> asks "who? what?"; the adjective <b>quī, quae, quod</b> asks "which? what (kind of)?": {Quis venit? = Who is coming?}{Quem librum legis? = Which book are you reading?}
<h4>Yes/no questions</h4><ul><li><b>-ne</b> on the first word: neutral. {Vidēsne? = Do you see?}</li><li><b>nōnne</b>: expects "yes". {Nōnne vidēs? = Surely you see? / You see, don't you?}</li><li><b>num</b>: expects "no". {Num timēs? = Surely you're not afraid?}</li><li><b>utrum … an</b>: "whether … or" (double question).</li></ul>
<h4>Question words</h4><p><span class=la>ubi</span> where · <span class=la>quō</span> where to · <span class=la>unde</span> from where · <span class=la>cūr</span> why · <span class=la>quandō</span> when · <span class=la>quōmodo</span> how · <span class=la>quot</span> how many · <span class=la>quantus</span> how big · <span class=la>quālis</span> of what kind.</p>
<p>Latin has no word for yes/no: answer by repeating the verb (<span class=la>videō</span> / <span class=la>nōn videō</span>) or use <span class=la>ita, vērō, sīc</span> / <span class=la>minimē</span>.</p>`,
q:[['A question with "num" expects…',['yes','no'],1,''],['"Quō vādis?" =',['Where are you going?','Who are you?'],0,''],['"Quem vīdistī?" =',['Whom did you see?','Which did you see?'],0,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Passive voice: present system',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.pres.P','ind.impf.P','ind.fut.P']},b:`<p>In the passive, the subject receives the action: "the city is captured". The agent (person) is <b>ā/ab + ablative</b>; the means/instrument is plain ablative.</p>
<table class=t><tr><th></th><th>Sg</th><th>Pl</th></tr><tr><td>1st</td><td>-or / -r</td><td>-mur</td></tr><tr><td>2nd</td><td>-ris</td><td>-minī</td></tr><tr><td>3rd</td><td>-tur</td><td>-ntur</td></tr></table>
[[V:amō|ind|P|pres,impf,fut]][[V:regō|ind|P|pres,impf,fut]]
<p>Watch 3rd conj.: <span class=la>regeris</span> (pres.) vs <span class=la>regēris</span> (fut.). Pres. passive infinitive: <span class=la>amārī, monērī, regī, capī, audīrī</span>.</p>
{Urbs ā mīlitibus capitur. = The city is being captured by the soldiers.}{Gladiō vulnerābātur. = He was being wounded with a sword.}`,
q:[['"laudābantur" =',['they were being praised','they will be praised'],0,''],['Agent "by the king" =',['rēge','ā rēge'],1,'People take ā/ab.'],['Present passive infinitive of "dūcō"?',['dūcerī','dūcī'],1,'3rd conj.: -ī.']]},
{u:2,t:'Passive voice: perfect system',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.perf.P','ind.plup.P','ind.futp.P']},b:`<p>The 4th principal part gives the <b>perfect passive participle</b> (PPP): <span class=la>amātum → amātus, -a, -um</span>. Perfect passives = PPP + a form of <b>sum</b>; the participle agrees with the subject.</p>[[V:amō|ind|P|perf,plup,futp]]
<p>Careful: <span class=la>amātus est</span> = "he was loved / has been loved" (perfect), not "he is loved". <span class=la>amātus erat</span> = "he had been loved".</p>
{Urbs capta est. = The city was captured. (capta fem. – urbs)}{Mīlitēs victī erant. = The soldiers had been conquered.}{Bellum gestum est. = The war was waged.}`,
q:[['"missī sunt" =',['they are sent','they were sent'],1,''],['"The letters had been written" =',['litterae scrīptae erant','litterae scrīptae sunt'],0,''],['Why "capta" in "urbs capta est"?',['agrees with fem. urbs','always -a'],0,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Imperatives & vocative',b:`<p>Commands. Singular = present stem; plural adds <b>-te</b> (3rd conj. <b>-ite</b>).</p><table class=t><tr><th></th><th>Sg</th><th>Pl</th></tr><tr><td>amō</td><td>amā</td><td>amāte</td></tr><tr><td>moneō</td><td>monē</td><td>monēte</td></tr><tr><td>regō</td><td>rege</td><td>regite</td></tr><tr><td>capiō</td><td>cape</td><td>capite</td></tr><tr><td>audiō</td><td>audī</td><td>audīte</td></tr></table>
<p>Irregular short forms: <b>dīc, dūc, fac, fer</b>. Esse: <span class=la>es, este</span>. Īre: <span class=la>ī, īte</span>.</p>
<h4>Negative commands</h4><p><b>nōlī / nōlīte + infinitive</b>: {Nōlī timēre! = Don't be afraid!} Also <span class=la>nē</span> + perfect subjunctive: <span class=la>nē timueris</span>.</p>
<p>Future imperative (laws, formal): <span class=la>-tō, -tōte, -ntō</span>: <span class=la>estō</span> "let him be".</p>
{Venī, Mārce, et audī! = Come, Marcus, and listen!}{Valē! Valēte! = Goodbye! (Be well!)}`,
q:[['Imperative pl. of "dūcō"?',['dūcite','dūcete'],0,''],['"Nōlīte currere!" =',['Don\'t run! (pl.)','Let\'s not run'],0,''],['Sg. imperative of "faciō"?',['face','fac'],1,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Irregular verbs',b:`<p>A handful of very common verbs are irregular. Learn them thoroughly – they're everywhere.</p>[[I:possum]][[I:eō]][[I:volō]][[I:nōlō]][[I:mālō]][[I:ferō]][[I:fīō]][[I:dō]]
<p>Perfect systems are regular from the 3rd principal part: <span class=la>tulī, tulistī…</span> <span class=la>potueram</span> etc.</p>`,
q:[['"vult" =',['he wants','he flies'],0,''],['"ībunt" =',['they went','they will go'],1,''],['"fit" =',['it happens / becomes','he makes'],0,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Numbers',b:`[[P:ūnus, duo, trēs]]<p>4–100 don't decline (<span class=la>quattuor, quīnque, sex, septem, octō, novem, decem … vīgintī</span> 20, <span class=la>centum</span> 100). Hundreds 200–900 decline like plural bonus (<span class=la>ducentī, -ae, -a</span>). <span class=la>mīlle</span> is indeclinable; plural <span class=la>mīlia</span> is a neuter noun + genitive: <span class=la>tria mīlia mīlitum</span> "3,000 soldiers".</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>#</th><th>Cardinal</th><th>Ordinal</th><th>Roman</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>ūnus</td><td>prīmus</td><td>I</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>duo</td><td>secundus</td><td>II</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>trēs</td><td>tertius</td><td>III</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>quattuor</td><td>quārtus</td><td>IV</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>quīnque</td><td>quīntus</td><td>V</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>sex</td><td>sextus</td><td>VI</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>septem</td><td>septimus</td><td>VII</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>octō</td><td>octāvus</td><td>VIII</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>novem</td><td>nōnus</td><td>IX</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>decem</td><td>decimus</td><td>X</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td>quīnquāgintā</td><td>quīnquāgēsimus</td><td>L</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>centum</td><td>centēsimus</td><td>C</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>quīngentī</td><td>quīngentēsimus</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>1000</td><td>mīlle</td><td>mīllēsimus</td><td>M</td></tr></table>
<p>Ordinals decline like bonus. Practise Roman numerals in the Drill tab.</p>`,
q:[['"duābus" is…',['dat./abl. fem. of duo','gen. of duo'],0,''],['XLIV =',['44','64','46'],0,''],['"2,000 soldiers" =',['duo mīlle mīlitēs','duo mīlia mīlitum'],1,'']]},
{u:2,t:'Comparison of adjectives & adverbs',b:`<h4>Comparative</h4><p>Stem + <b>-ior</b> (m/f), <b>-ius</b> (n); gen. <b>-iōris</b>; declined as a consonant-stem 3rd declension (abl. -e, gen. pl. -um, n. pl. -a). <span class=la>longior, longius</span> "longer, rather long, too long".</p>
<h4>Superlative</h4><p>Stem + <b>-issimus, -a, -um</b>: <span class=la>longissimus</span> "longest, very long". Adjectives in <b>-er</b>: <b>-rimus</b> (<span class=la>pulcherrimus, ācerrimus</span>). Six in <b>-lis</b>: <b>-limus</b> (<span class=la>facillimus, simillimus, difficillimus</span>).</p>
<h4>Irregular</h4><table class=t><tr><td>bonus</td><td>melior</td><td>optimus</td></tr><tr><td>malus</td><td>peior</td><td>pessimus</td></tr><tr><td>magnus</td><td>maior</td><td>maximus</td></tr><tr><td>parvus</td><td>minor</td><td>minimus</td></tr><tr><td>multus</td><td>plūs (n. noun)</td><td>plūrimus</td></tr><tr><td>(prō)</td><td>prior</td><td>prīmus</td></tr><tr><td>superus</td><td>superior</td><td>summus / suprēmus</td></tr></table>
<h4>"than"</h4><p><b>quam</b> + same case, or the <b>ablative of comparison</b> (no quam): {Melior est quam frāter. = He is better than his brother.}{Melior est frātre. = (same)} <b>quam + superlative</b> = "as … as possible": <span class=la>quam celerrimē</span>.</p>
<h4>Adverbs</h4><p>From 1st/2nd adj.: <b>-ē</b> (<span class=la>lātē</span>); from 3rd: <b>-iter</b> (<span class=la>fortiter</span>). Comparative adverb = neuter comparative (<span class=la>fortius</span>); superlative -issimē (<span class=la>fortissimē</span>). Irregular: <span class=la>bene, melius, optimē; male, peius, pessimē; magnopere, magis, maximē; diū, diūtius, diūtissimē</span>.</p>`,
q:[['Superlative of "facilis"?',['facilissimus','facillimus'],1,''],['"Nihil est dulcius melle" =',['Nothing is sweeter than honey','Nothing is sweet with honey'],0,'Abl. of comparison.'],['Adverb of "ācer"?',['ācrē','ācriter'],1,'3rd-decl. adj. → -iter.']]},
{u:3,t:'Infinitives',b:`<p>Latin has six infinitives:</p><table class=t><tr><th></th><th>Active</th><th>Passive</th></tr><tr><td>Present</td><td>amāre to love</td><td>amārī to be loved</td></tr><tr><td>Perfect</td><td>amāvisse to have loved</td><td>amātus esse to have been loved</td></tr><tr><td>Future</td><td>amātūrus esse to be about to love</td><td>(amātum īrī – rare)</td></tr></table>[[NF:regō]]
<h4>Uses</h4><ul><li><b>Complementary</b> (with possum, volō, dēbeō, incipiō, cōnstituō…): {Īre volō. = I want to go.}</li><li><b>Subject</b> (a neuter noun): {Errāre hūmānum est. = To err is human.}</li><li><b>Object</b> with iubeō, vetō, patior: {Mīlitēs pugnāre iussit. = He ordered the soldiers to fight.}</li><li><b>Indirect statement</b> – lesson 28, the most important use.</li><li><b>Historical infinitive</b> (vivid narrative) – translate as a past tense.</li></ul>`,
q:[['"captus esse" =',['to have been captured','to capture'],0,''],['Future active infinitive of "veniō"?',['ventūrus esse','venīrī'],0,''],['"Vincere potest" =',['He can conquer','He conquered'],0,'']]},
{u:3,t:'Participles',b:`<p>Participles are verbal adjectives. Latin has four:</p><table class=t><tr><th>Participle</th><th>Form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Present active</td><td>amāns, amantis (3rd decl.)</td><td>loving</td></tr><tr><td>Perfect passive</td><td>amātus, -a, -um</td><td>(having been) loved</td></tr><tr><td>Future active</td><td>amātūrus, -a, -um</td><td>about to love, going to love</td></tr><tr><td>Gerundive (fut. passive)</td><td>amandus, -a, -um</td><td>to be loved, needing to be loved</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Time is relative to the main verb</b>: present = same time; perfect = before; future = after.</p>
{Mīles vulnerātus fūgit. = The wounded soldier fled. / The soldier, having been wounded, fled.}{Puerum clāmantem audīvimus. = We heard the boy shouting.}{Moritūrī tē salūtant. = Those about to die salute you.}
<p>Translate flexibly with clauses: "when / since / although / who…". {Hostēs urbem captam incendērunt. = The enemy captured the city and burnt it. (lit. burnt the captured city)}</p>
<p>Latin has <b>no perfect active participle</b> for normal verbs – except deponents (lesson 29): <span class=la>locūtus</span> "having spoken".</p>`,
q:[['"vocātus" =',['calling','having been called','about to call'],1,''],['Present participle of "audiō"?',['audāns','audiēns'],1,''],['"ventūrus" =',['about to come','having come'],0,'']]},
{u:3,t:'Ablative absolute',b:`<p>A noun/pronoun + participle in the <b>ablative</b>, grammatically separate from the rest of the sentence, giving background (time, cause, condition, concession).</p>
{Urbe captā, hostēs discessērunt. = With the city captured / After the city was captured, the enemy departed.}{Caesare duce, vincēmus. = With Caesar as leader, we will win.}{Sōle oriente, profectī sunt. = As the sun was rising, they set out.}
<p>Steps: (1) spot noun + participle both in the ablative, not after a preposition; (2) translate literally "with X having been …ed"; (3) turn into a natural clause (when, after, since, although, if).</p>
<p>Since <span class=la>sum</span> has no present participle, two nouns or noun + adjective can form one: <span class=la>Cicerōne cōnsule</span> "when Cicero was consul".</p>
<p>The subject of the ablative absolute is normally <b>not</b> the subject or object of the main clause.</p>`,
q:[['"Rēge interfectō…" =',['After the king was killed…','The king killed…'],0,''],['"mē invītō" =',['against my will','I invited'],0,'"with me unwilling"'],['Case of an ablative absolute?',['Ablative','Nominative'],0,'']]},
{u:3,t:'Indirect statement',b:`<p>After verbs of <b>saying, thinking, knowing, perceiving</b> (dīcō, putō, sciō, sentiō, videō, audiō, nūntiō, crēdō…), Latin uses <b>accusative + infinitive</b> – no word for "that".</p>
{Dīcit puellam venīre. = He says that the girl is coming.}{Dīxit puellam venīre. = He said that the girl was coming.}{Dīcit puellam vēnisse. = He says that the girl came.}{Dīcit puellam ventūram esse. = He says that the girl will come.}
<p><b>Infinitive tense is relative</b>: present = same time as main verb; perfect = before; future = after. Then adjust English tense to the main verb.</p>
<p>Reflexive <span class=la>sē</span> = the main subject: {Dīcit sē fessum esse. = He says that he (himself) is tired.}{Dīcit eum fessum esse. = He says that he (someone else) is tired.}
<p><b>negō</b> = "say that … not": <span class=la>negat sē vēnisse</span> "he says he did not come". Participles in compound infinitives agree with the accusative subject.</p>`,
q:[['"Scīmus tē esse fortem" =',['We know that you are brave','We know you to be braver'],0,''],['"Putāvit hostēs fūgisse" =',['He thought the enemy had fled','He thought the enemy were fleeing'],0,'Perfect inf. = before.'],['Main subject in indirect statement is shown by…',['sē','eum'],0,'']]},
{u:3,t:'Deponent & semi-deponent verbs',dr:{k:'verb',t:['ind.pres.P','ind.perf.P'],dep:true},b:`<p><b>Deponent</b> verbs have <b>passive forms but active meanings</b>. Only three principal parts: <span class=la>hortor, hortārī, hortātus sum</span> "encourage".</p>[[V:hortor|ind|P|pres,impf,fut,perf]]
{Mīlitēs hortātur. = He encourages the soldiers.}{Rōmam profectus est. = He set out for Rome.}
<p>Deponents keep active forms for: present & future participles (<span class=la>hortāns, hortātūrus</span>), future infinitive, gerund. Their perfect participle is <b>active</b>: <span class=la>locūtus</span> "having spoken". The gerundive stays passive: <span class=la>hortandus</span> "to be encouraged".</p>
<p>Imperative: <span class=la>hortāre!</span> (sg.), <span class=la>hortāminī!</span> (pl.); <span class=la>sequere mē!</span> "follow me!"</p>
<p>Five deponents take the <b>ablative</b>: <span class=la>ūtor, fruor, fungor, potior, vēscor</span> ("use, enjoy, perform, gain, feed on").</p>
<h4>Semi-deponents</h4><p>Active in the present system, passive in the perfect: <span class=la>audeō, audēre, ausus sum</span> "dare"; <span class=la>gaudeō, gāvīsus sum</span>; <span class=la>soleō, solitus sum</span> "be accustomed"; <span class=la>fīdō, fīsus sum</span> "trust".</p>`,
q:[['"loquitur" =',['he speaks','he is spoken'],0,''],['"secūtus" =',['having followed','having been followed'],0,''],['"Gladiō ūtitur" – why gladiō?',['ūtor takes the ablative','object of a preposition'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Subjunctive: forms',dr:{k:'verb',t:['sub.pres.A','sub.impf.A','sub.perf.A','sub.plup.A']},b:`<p>The subjunctive is the mood of the possible, wished, intended or dependent. It has four tenses (no future).</p>
<h4>Present: change the vowel</h4><p>"<b>We beat a giant liar</b>": 1st <b>-e-</b> (amem); 2nd <b>-ea-</b> (moneam); 3rd <b>-a-</b> (regam); 3rd -iō / 4th <b>-ia-</b> (capiam, audiam).</p>[[V:amō|sub|A|pres,impf,perf,plup]]
<p><b>Imperfect</b>: present active infinitive + endings: <span class=la>amāre-m, regere-m, esse-m</span>. Easy to spot!</p><p><b>Perfect</b>: perfect stem + <b>-erim</b> (like fut. perf. but 1st sg. -erim). <b>Pluperfect</b>: perfect active infinitive + endings: <span class=la>amāvisse-m</span>.</p>
[[V:regō|sub|A|pres,impf]][[V:regō|sub|P|pres,impf,perf,plup]]
<p>Passive: same patterns with passive endings; perfect = PPP + <span class=la>sim</span>, pluperfect = PPP + <span class=la>essem</span>.</p>`,
q:[['Present subjunctive of "amō" (3 sg.)?',['amat','amet'],1,''],['"regeret" is…',['impf. subj.','pres. ind.'],0,'Infinitive + t.'],['"audīvissent" is…',['pluperf. subj.','perf. ind.'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Independent subjunctives',b:`<p>A subjunctive as the main verb expresses will, wish or possibility.</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th><th>Neg.</th></tr><tr><td><b>Hortatory</b> (1st pl.)</td><td>Vīvāmus! "Let us live!"</td><td>nē</td></tr><tr><td><b>Jussive</b> (3rd)</td><td>Veniat! "Let him come!"</td><td>nē</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prohibition</b></td><td>Nē timueris! "Don't fear!" (perf. subj.)</td><td>nē</td></tr><tr><td><b>Deliberative</b></td><td>Quid faciam? "What am I to do?"</td><td>nōn</td></tr><tr><td><b>Optative</b> (wish)</td><td>Utinam veniat! "May he come!"<br>Utinam adesset! "If only he were here!" (impf. = present unfulfilled)<br>Utinam vēnisset! "If only he had come!" (plup. = past unfulfilled)</td><td>nē</td></tr><tr><td><b>Potential</b></td><td>Aliquis dīcat… "Someone may/might say…"</td><td>nōn</td></tr></table>
{Gaudeāmus igitur! = Let us rejoice, therefore!}{Valeat quantum valēre potest. = Let it count for what it can.}`,
q:[['"Eāmus!" =',['Let\'s go!','We went'],0,'Hortatory.'],['"Utinam nē vēnissent!" =',['If only they hadn\'t come!','May they come!'],0,''],['"Quid dīcam?" =',['What am I to say?','What did I say?'],0,'Deliberative.']]},
{u:4,t:'Purpose clauses',b:`<p>"in order to / so that": <b>ut</b> + subjunctive; negative <b>nē</b>. English often uses a simple infinitive – Latin never does in prose.</p>
{Vēnit ut mē vidēret. = He came to see me.}{Fūgērunt nē caperentur. = They fled so as not to be captured.}
<p><b>quō</b> replaces ut when the clause contains a comparative: <span class=la>quō facilius…</span> "so that more easily…".</p>
<h4>Other ways to say purpose</h4><ul><li><b>Relative purpose</b>: {Lēgātōs mīsit quī pācem peterent. = He sent envoys to seek peace.}</li><li><b>ad + gerund(ive)</b> (acc.): <span class=la>ad pācem petendam</span></li><li><b>causā / grātiā + gen. gerund(ive)</b>: <span class=la>pācis petendae causā</span></li><li><b>Supine in -um</b> after verbs of motion: <span class=la>vēnērunt pācem petītum</span>.</li><li><b>Future participle</b>: <span class=la>pācem petītūrī</span>.</li></ul>`,
q:[['"…nē vidērētur" =',['so that he would not be seen','so that he was seen'],0,''],['"Mīsit servum quī cibum emeret" =',['He sent a slave to buy food','He sent the slave who bought food'],0,'Relative purpose (subjunctive).'],['Purpose negative?',['ut nōn','nē'],1,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Result clauses',b:`<p>"so … that": <b>ut</b> + subjunctive, negative <b>ut nōn</b>. Usually signalled by a "so" word in the main clause:</p><p><span class=la>tam</span> so · <span class=la>ita, sīc</span> so, in such a way · <span class=la>tantus</span> so great · <span class=la>tālis</span> such · <span class=la>tot</span> so many · <span class=la>adeō</span> to such an extent · <span class=la>tantopere</span> so greatly.</p>
{Tanta erat tempestās ut nāvēs dēlērentur. = The storm was so great that the ships were destroyed.}{Tam celeriter cucurrit ut nēmō eum caperet. = He ran so fast that no one caught him.}{Ita dīxit ut omnēs eum audīre nōn possent. = He spoke so that not all could hear him.}
<p><b>Purpose vs result</b>: same "ut + subj.", but result usually has a signpost word, and the negative is <b>ut nōn / ut nēmō / ut nihil</b> (purpose uses nē, nē quis, nē quid).</p>
<p><b>Noun result clauses</b> after <span class=la>accidit, fit, efficiō</span>: {Accidit ut lūna plēna esset. = It happened that the moon was full.}</p>`,
q:[['Negative of a result clause?',['nē','ut nōn'],1,''],['Signal word for result?',['tam','quod'],0,''],['"Fit ut…" introduces…',['a noun result clause','a purpose clause'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Cum clauses',b:`<p><b>cum</b> + indicative = "when" (strictly time). <b>cum + subjunctive</b> describes circumstances:</p>
<ul><li><b>Circumstantial</b> "when": {Cum Caesar advēnisset, hostēs fūgērunt. = When Caesar had arrived, the enemy fled.}</li><li><b>Causal</b> "since": {Cum sīs fortis, nōn timēs. = Since you are brave, you are not afraid.}</li><li><b>Concessive</b> "although" (often with <span class=la>tamen</span> in main clause): {Cum dīves esset, tamen miser erat. = Although he was rich, he was miserable.}</li></ul>
<p>Tense: impf. subj. = same time ("when he was…"); plup. subj. = before ("when he had…").</p>
<h4>Other temporal & causal conjunctions</h4><p><span class=la>postquam, ubi, ut, simul ac</span> ("after, when, as soon as") + <b>perfect indicative</b> (translate as pluperfect). <span class=la>dum</span> "while" + present indicative (even of past time). <span class=la>dum, antequam, priusquam</span> "until / before" + subjunctive when anticipation/purpose is implied. <span class=la>quod, quia, quoniam</span> "because" + indicative (subjunctive = alleged reason).</p>`,
q:[['"Cum hoc vīdisset, discessit" =',['When he had seen this, he left','Since he sees this, he leaves'],0,''],['Signal of concessive cum?',['tamen','quod'],0,''],['"Dum haec geruntur…" =',['While these things were happening…','Until these things happen'],0,'dum + present indic.']]},
{u:4,t:'Indirect questions & sequence of tenses',b:`<p>A question reported after a verb of asking, knowing, saying etc. uses the <b>subjunctive</b>: {Rogat quid faciās. = He asks what you are doing.}{Nescio ubi sit. = I don't know where he is.}
<h4>Sequence of tenses</h4><p>The tense of a dependent subjunctive follows the main verb:</p><table class=t><tr><th>Main verb</th><th>Same time / after</th><th>Before</th></tr><tr><td><b>Primary</b>: present, future, fut. perf. (perf. = "have")</td><td>Present subj.</td><td>Perfect subj.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Secondary</b>: imperfect, perfect, pluperfect</td><td>Imperfect subj.</td><td>Pluperfect subj.</td></tr></table>
{Rogō quid faciās / fēcerīs. = I ask what you are doing / did.}{Rogāvī quid facerēs / fēcissēs. = I asked what you were doing / had done.}
<p>For a later action in indirect questions: future participle + sim / essem: {Nescit quid factūrus sim. = He doesn't know what I will do.}</p>
<p>Purpose and result clauses follow sequence too (using present/imperfect).</p>`,
q:[['"Scīvit cūr vēnissēmus" =',['He knew why we had come','He knows why we came'],0,''],['After a primary main verb, "same time" uses…',['pres. subj.','impf. subj.'],0,''],['"Quaerō num veniat" =',['I ask whether he is coming','I ask him to come'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Indirect commands',b:`<p>A command reported after verbs of ordering, asking, urging, persuading: <b>ut / nē + subjunctive</b>, following sequence of tenses.</p>
<p>Verbs: <span class=la>imperō</span> (+dat.), <span class=la>persuādeō</span> (+dat.), <span class=la>moneō, hortor, rogō, ōrō, petō</span> (ā + abl.), <span class=la>postulō</span>.</p>
{Imperāvit mīlitibus ut pugnārent. = He ordered the soldiers to fight.}{Mē hortātus est nē discēderem. = He urged me not to leave.}{Eī persuāsit ut venīret. = He persuaded him to come.}
<p><b>Exception:</b> <span class=la>iubeō</span> and <span class=la>vetō</span> ("forbid") take accusative + infinitive: <span class=la>eōs īre iussit</span>.</p>`,
q:[['"Rogāvit ut manērem" =',['He asked me to stay','He asked whether I stayed'],0,''],['Which takes acc. + inf.?',['iubeō','imperō'],0,''],['Negative indirect command?',['nē','nōn'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Conditional sentences',b:`<table class=t><tr><th>Type</th><th>Latin</th><th>Example</th></tr>
<tr><td>Simple (fact)</td><td>indicative, any tense</td><td>Sī hoc dīcis, errās. "If you say this, you are wrong."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Future more vivid</td><td>fut. / fut. perf. indic.</td><td>Sī vēneris, gaudēbō. "If you come, I'll be glad."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Future less vivid ("should … would")</td><td>pres. subj.</td><td>Sī veniās, gaudeam. "If you should come, I would be glad."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Present contrary to fact</td><td>impf. subj.</td><td>Sī hīc essēs, laetus essem. "If you were here, I would be happy."</td></tr>
<tr><td>Past contrary to fact</td><td>plup. subj.</td><td>Sī vēnissēs, tē vīdissem. "If you had come, I would have seen you."</td></tr></table>
<p>Mixed conditions are common: plup. subj. in "if" clause + impf. subj. in main clause ("if you had…, I would now…").</p>
<p><b>nisi</b> = "unless / if not"; <span class=la>sī nōn</span> negates one word. <span class=la>sīve … sīve</span> = "whether … or".</p>`,
q:[['"Sī audīrem, respondērem" =',['If I heard, I would answer (now)','If I hear, I will answer'],0,'Impf. subj. = present contrary to fact.'],['Past contrary to fact uses…',['plup. subj.','perf. indic.'],0,''],['"nisi" =',['unless','whether'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Fear clauses; quīn & quōminus',b:`<h4>Fear clauses</h4><p>After verbs of fearing (<span class=la>timeō, metuō, vereor</span>; <span class=la>perīculum est</span>): <b>nē</b> + subj. = "that/lest"; <b>ut</b> (or nē nōn) = "that … not". Yes, it looks backwards: Latin thinks "I fear: may it not happen!"</p>
{Timeō nē hostēs veniant. = I fear that the enemy may come.}{Timeō ut veniat. = I fear that he may not come.}
<h4>quōminus</h4><p>After verbs of hindering/preventing (<span class=la>impediō, dēterreō, obstō</span>): {Tempestās impedīvit quōminus nāvigārēmus. = The storm prevented us from sailing.}</p>
<h4>quīn</h4><p>After <b>negative</b> expressions of doubt/hindering: {Nōn dubitō quīn vērum dīcās. = I don't doubt that you are telling the truth.}{Nōn multum āfuit quīn caperētur. = He came close to being captured.} <span class=la>Facere nōn possum quīn…</span> "I can't help …ing".</p>`,
q:[['"Vereor nē cadat" =',['I fear that he may fall','I fear that he may not fall'],0,''],['"Timeō ut veniat" =',['I fear he may not come','I fear he will come'],0,''],['quīn follows a…',['negative expression of doubt','positive command'],0,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Gerund, gerundive & passive periphrastic',b:`<p>The <b>gerund</b> is a verbal noun ("…ing"), neuter singular only: gen. <span class=la>amandī</span>, dat. <span class=la>amandō</span>, acc. <span class=la>(ad) amandum</span>, abl. <span class=la>amandō</span>. (For the nominative use the infinitive.)</p>
{ars scrībendī = the art of writing}{Discimus legendō. = We learn by reading.}
<p>The <b>gerundive</b> is a passive verbal adjective (<span class=la>amandus, -a, -um</span>). Latin prefers a gerundive phrase to a gerund with an object:</p>{ad urbem capiendam = to capture the city (lit. for the city to be captured)}{lēgis ferendae causā = for the sake of passing the law}
<h4>Passive periphrastic: obligation</h4><p>Gerundive + <b>sum</b> = "must be …ed"; the person who must do it is in the <b>dative of agent</b>:</p>{Carthāgō dēlenda est. = Carthage must be destroyed.}{Urbs nōbīs dēfendenda est. = We must defend the city. (lit. the city is to be defended by us)}{Mihi eundum est. = I must go. (impersonal, intransitive verb)}
<p>The <b>active periphrastic</b> = future participle + sum: <span class=la>pugnātūrus sum</span> "I am about to fight".</p>`,
q:[['"Hoc tibi faciendum est" =',['You must do this','You are doing this'],0,''],['"spēs vincendī" =',['hope of winning','hope to be won'],0,''],['Agent with a gerundive of obligation?',['ā + abl.','dative'],1,'']]},
{u:4,t:'Relative clauses with the subjunctive',b:`<p>Relative clauses normally take the indicative. The <b>subjunctive</b> shows extra meaning:</p>
<ul><li><b>Characteristic</b> (describes the sort of person/thing), after <span class=la>sunt quī, nēmō est quī, sōlus, dignus est quī</span>: {Sunt quī dīcant… = There are people who would say…}{Nēmō est quī hoc nesciat. = There is no one who doesn't know this.}</li><li><b>Purpose</b> (lesson 32): {Mīsit quī rogārent. = He sent men to ask.}</li><li><b>Causal / concessive</b> (often with <span class=la>quippe, utpote</span>): {Ō fortūnāte adulēscēns, quī Homērum praecōnem invēneris! = O lucky youth, since you found Homer as herald!}</li><li><b>Result</b>: <span class=la>dignus est quī laudētur</span> "he is worthy to be praised".</li></ul>`,
q:[['"Nēmō est quī nōn timeat" =',['There is no one who is not afraid','No one is afraid'],0,''],['"Dignus est quī regat" =',['He is worthy to rule','He rules worthily'],0,''],['Which uses the subjunctive?',['Relative clause of characteristic','Ordinary descriptive relative'],0,'']]},
{u:5,t:'Uses of the genitive',b:`<ul><li><b>Possession</b>: <span class=la>domus patris</span> "father's house"</li><li><b>Partitive</b> (of the whole): <span class=la>pars mīlitum</span>, <span class=la>satis cibī</span> "enough (of) food", <span class=la>nihil novī</span> "nothing new", <span class=la>quis vestrum</span></li><li><b>Description / quality</b> (with adjective): <span class=la>vir magnae virtūtis</span> "a man of great courage"</li><li><b>Objective</b>: <span class=la>amor patriae</span> "love for the country"; <b>subjective</b>: <span class=la>amor mātris</span> "a mother's love"</li><li><b>Value</b>: <span class=la>magnī aestimāre</span> "value highly", <span class=la>parvī, nihilī</span></li><li><b>Charge</b> (with accuse/condemn): <span class=la>prōditiōnis damnātus</span></li><li>With <b>adjectives</b> (plēnus, cupidus, perītus, memor, similis): <span class=la>cupidus glōriae</span></li><li>With <b>verbs</b> of remembering/forgetting (<span class=la>meminī, oblīvīscor</span>), pitying (<span class=la>misereor</span>)</li><li>With <b>causā / grātiā</b> (placed after): <span class=la>honōris causā</span></li><li><b>Characteristic</b> with esse: {Est rēgis lēgēs dare. = It is the mark of a king to give laws.}</li></ul>`,
q:[['"magnī aestimāre" =',['value highly','estimate the great'],0,'Gen. of value.'],['"satis pecūniae" – what genitive?',['partitive','possessive'],0,''],['"vir summae prūdentiae" =',['a man of the greatest prudence','the prudence of a man'],0,'']]},
{u:5,t:'Uses of the dative',b:`<ul><li><b>Indirect object</b>: <span class=la>librum puerō dō</span></li><li><b>Reference / advantage</b>: {Nōn scholae sed vītae discimus. = We learn not for school but for life.}</li><li><b>Possession</b> with esse: {Mihi est liber. = I have a book. (lit. there is a book to me)}</li><li><b>Purpose</b>: <span class=la>auxiliō mittere</span> "send as help"; <b>double dative</b> (purpose + reference): {Haec rēs mihi cūrae est. = This matter is a concern to me.}</li><li><b>Agent</b> with gerundive: <span class=la>mihi faciendum</span></li><li>With <b>special intransitive verbs</b>: <span class=la>crēdō, faveō, ignōscō, imperō, noceō, parcō, pāreō, persuādeō, placeō, resistō, serviō, studeō</span> – {Mihi crēde. = Believe me.}</li><li>With <b>compound verbs</b> (ad-, ante-, con-, in-, inter-, ob-, prae-, sub-, super-): <span class=la>bellum cīvibus īnferre</span></li><li>With <b>adjectives</b> of nearness, likeness, friendliness: <span class=la>amīcus, similis, fidēlis, proximus</span></li><li><b>Separation</b> with verbs of taking away: <span class=la>scūtum mīlitī dētrāxit</span></li></ul>`,
q:[['"Est mihi soror" =',['I have a sister','My sister is'],0,'Dat. of possession.'],['"Parce mihi!" – why mihi?',['parcō takes the dative','object of a preposition'],0,''],['"praesidiō mittere" =',['to send as a guard','to send a guard away'],0,'Dat. of purpose.']]},
{u:5,t:'Uses of the accusative',b:`<ul><li><b>Direct object</b></li><li><b>Motion toward</b> (with ad, in; or alone with cities, small islands, domus, rūs): <span class=la>Rōmam īre</span> "to go to Rome", <span class=la>domum</span> "home(wards)"</li><li><b>Extent of time</b>: {Tōtam noctem dormīvit. = He slept the whole night.}</li><li><b>Extent of space</b>: <span class=la>mūrus decem pedēs altus</span> "a wall ten feet high"</li><li><b>Subject of an infinitive</b> (indirect statement)</li><li><b>Double accusative</b> with verbs of asking, teaching, naming, making: {Puerōs litterās docet. = He teaches the boys literature.}{Eum rēgem appellant. = They call him king.}</li><li><b>Exclamation</b>: <span class=la>Ō mē miserum!</span> "Oh wretched me!"</li><li><b>Respect</b> (Greek/poetic): <span class=la>nūda pedem</span> "bare as to the foot"</li><li><b>Adverbial</b>: <span class=la>multum, nihil, plūrimum</span></li></ul>`,
q:[['"Trēs annōs mānsit" =',['He stayed for three years','He stayed in the third year'],0,'Extent of time.'],['"Athēnās nāvigāvit" =',['He sailed to Athens','He sailed from Athens'],0,''],['"Ō mē miserum!" – mē miserum is accusative of…',['exclamation','direct object'],0,'']]},
{u:5,t:'Uses of the ablative',b:`<p>The ablative merges three old cases: <b>from</b> (separation), <b>with</b> (instrument, accompaniment), <b>in/at</b> (location).</p>
<ul><li><b>Means / instrument</b> (no prep.): <span class=la>gladiō vulnerātus</span></li><li><b>Agent</b> (ā/ab + person) with passive verbs</li><li><b>Accompaniment</b> (cum): <span class=la>cum amīcīs</span></li><li><b>Manner</b> (cum, optional if an adj.): <span class=la>(cum) magnā cūrā</span>, <span class=la>silentiō</span></li><li><b>Place where</b> (in): <span class=la>in urbe</span></li><li><b>Place from which</b> (ā, ē, dē; alone with cities, domus): <span class=la>Rōmā</span> "from Rome"</li><li><b>Separation</b>: <span class=la>līberāre perīculō</span></li><li><b>Time when / within which</b>: <span class=la>prīmā lūce</span> "at dawn", <span class=la>tribus diēbus</span> "within three days"</li><li><b>Cause</b>: <span class=la>timōre fūgērunt</span></li><li><b>Comparison</b>: <span class=la>altior monte</span></li><li><b>Degree of difference</b>: <span class=la>multō maior</span> "much bigger", <span class=la>paucīs post diēbus</span> "a few days later"</li><li><b>Description</b>: <span class=la>vir magnā virtūte</span></li><li><b>Respect / specification</b>: <span class=la>virtūte praestāre</span> "excel in courage"</li><li><b>Price</b>: <span class=la>magnō emere</span></li><li><b>Origin</b>: <span class=la>nōbilī genere nātus</span></li><li>With <b>ūtor, fruor, fungor, potior, vēscor</b>, and <b>dignus</b></li><li><b>Ablative absolute</b></li></ul>`,
q:[['"multō celerior" – multō is abl. of…',['degree of difference','means'],0,''],['"prīmā lūce" =',['at dawn','by the first light'],0,'Time when.'],['"Rōmā vēnit" =',['He came from Rome','He came to Rome'],0,'']]},
{u:5,t:'Place & time; the locative',b:`<table class=t><tr><th></th><th>Normal nouns</th><th>Cities, small islands, domus, rūs</th></tr><tr><td>Place to which</td><td>ad/in + acc.</td><td>acc. alone: Rōmam, domum, rūs</td></tr><tr><td>Place from which</td><td>ā/ē/dē + abl.</td><td>abl. alone: Rōmā, domō, rūre</td></tr><tr><td>Place where</td><td>in + abl.</td><td><b>locative</b>: Rōmae, domī, rūrī</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Locative forms</b>: 1st & 2nd sg. = genitive (<span class=la>Rōmae, Corinthī</span>); 3rd & pl. = ablative (<span class=la>Carthāgine, Athēnīs</span>). Also <span class=la>humī</span> "on the ground", <span class=la>mīlitiae</span> "on campaign".</p>
<table class=t><tr><th>Time</th><th>Case</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>when</td><td>abl.</td><td>eō diē "on that day"</td></tr><tr><td>within which</td><td>abl.</td><td>paucīs hōrīs "within a few hours"</td></tr><tr><td>how long</td><td>acc.</td><td>multōs annōs "for many years"</td></tr><tr><td>ago / later</td><td>abhinc + acc.; abl. + post/ante</td><td>tribus annīs post "three years later"</td></tr></table>`,
q:[['"at Athens" =',['in Athēnīs','Athēnīs'],1,'Locative (= abl. pl.).'],['"Domum rediit" =',['He returned home','He returned from home'],0,''],['"at Corinth" =',['Corinthī','Corinthō'],0,'2nd decl. sg. locative = gen.']]},
{u:6,t:'Impersonal verbs',b:`<p>Verbs used only in the 3rd sg. with "it" (or no) subject.</p>
<ul><li><b>Weather</b>: <span class=la>pluit</span> "it rains", <span class=la>ningit</span> "it snows", <span class=la>tonat</span></li><li><b>Feeling</b> (acc. of person + gen. of cause): <span class=la>mē miseret, paenitet, piget, pudet, taedet</span> – {Mē pudet stultitiae meae. = I am ashamed of my folly.}</li><li><b>licet</b> (dat. + inf.): {Tibi īre licet. = You are allowed to go.} <b>placet</b>: "it seems good, is decided"</li><li><b>oportet</b> (acc. + inf.): {Tē hoc facere oportet. = You ought to do this.} <b>decet</b> "it is fitting"</li><li><b>necesse est, opus est</b> (+ dat.), <b>interest / rēfert</b> "it concerns" (meā interest)</li><li><b>accidit, fit, ēvenit</b> + ut "it happens that"; <b>cōnstat</b> "it is agreed"</li><li><b>Impersonal passive</b> of intransitive verbs: {Pugnātum est. = There was fighting / A battle was fought.}{Ventum est. = They came.}</li></ul>`,
q:[['"Mē taedet vītae" =',['I am tired of life','Life bores him'],0,''],['"Licet nōbīs" =',['We are allowed','We like'],0,''],['"Diū pugnātum est" =',['There was fighting for a long time','He fought for a long time'],0,'']]},
{u:6,t:'Oratio obliqua (extended indirect speech)',b:`<p>Historians report whole speeches indirectly. Rules:</p>
<ul><li>Main <b>statements</b> → acc. + infinitive.</li><li>Main <b>commands</b> → subjunctive (no ut): <span class=la>…mīlitēs fortiter pugnārent</span> "(he said) the soldiers should fight bravely".</li><li>Real <b>questions</b> → subjunctive; rhetorical questions → acc. + inf.</li><li>All <b>subordinate clauses</b> → subjunctive, following sequence.</li><li>Pronouns shift: 1st person → <span class=la>sē, suus</span>; 2nd → <span class=la>ille, is</span>.</li></ul>
{Direct: "Sī vēneris, tē laudābō." = "If you come, I will praise you."}{Indirect: Dīxit, sī ille vēnisset, sē eum laudātūrum esse. = He said that if he came, he would praise him.}
<p><b>Subjunctive by attraction</b>: a clause inside a subjunctive or infinitive clause often becomes subjunctive too. <b>Virtual oratio obliqua</b>: subjunctive in a quod-clause shows the reason is someone's claim, not the writer's: <span class=la>laudat eum quod vēnerit</span> "he praises him because (as he says) he came".</p>`,
q:[['In oratio obliqua, subordinate clauses use…',['the subjunctive','the indicative'],0,''],['A command in oratio obliqua becomes…',['subjunctive','imperative'],0,''],['"quod + subj." suggests…',['an alleged reason','a fact'],0,'']]},
{u:6,t:'Word order & style',b:`<p>Latin word order is free but meaningful. First and last positions are emphatic.</p>
<ul><li><b>Hyperbaton</b>: separating words that belong together: <span class=la>magnā cum laude</span>; <span class=la>Gallia est omnis dīvīsa</span>.</li><li><b>Chiasmus</b> (ABBA): <span class=la>Satis ēloquentiae, sapientiae parum</span>.</li><li><b>Anaphora</b>: repetition at the start: <span class=la>Nihil… nihil… nihil…</span></li><li><b>Asyndeton</b>: no conjunctions: <span class=la>Vēnī, vīdī, vīcī</span>.</li><li><b>Polysyndeton</b>: many conjunctions.</li><li><b>Tricolon</b> (often crescendo): three parallel units growing longer.</li><li><b>Litotes</b>: understatement by negation: <span class=la>nōn ignōbilis</span> = famous.</li><li><b>Ellipsis</b>: omission, esp. of <span class=la>est/sunt</span>.</li><li><b>Golden line</b> (poetry): adj. A, adj. B, verb, noun A, noun B.</li><li><b>Periodic sentence</b>: subordinate clauses first, main verb last – keeps you waiting.</li></ul>
<h4>Reading strategy</h4><ol><li>Read the whole sentence in Latin order; note case & mood of every word.</li><li>Find the main verb, then its subject.</li><li>Bracket subordinate clauses and participle phrases.</li><li>Pair adjectives with nouns by gender/number/case.</li><li>Translate, then rephrase into natural English.</li></ol>`,
q:[['"Vēnī, vīdī, vīcī" shows…',['asyndeton','polysyndeton'],0,''],['ABBA arrangement =',['chiasmus','anaphora'],0,''],['"nōn ignārus" (= well aware) is…',['litotes','hyperbaton'],0,'']]},
{u:6,t:'Meter: hexameter & elegiac couplet',b:`<p>Latin verse is based on <b>syllable length</b>, not stress. A syllable is <b>long</b> if it has a long vowel or diphthong, or a short vowel followed by two consonants (x = cs, z count as two; but a mute + l/r may count as one).</p>
<h4>Dactylic hexameter</h4><p>Six feet: dactyls (– ∪ ∪) or spondees (– –); the 5th is almost always a dactyl, the 6th – x. Used by Vergil and Ovid's Metamorphoses.</p>
<p class=mono>Ār-ma vi | rum-que ca | nō Trō | iae quī | prī-mus ab | ō-rīs<br>– ∪ ∪ | – ∪ ∪ | – – | – – | – ∪ ∪ | – –</p>
<h4>Elision</h4><p>A final vowel (or vowel + m) is dropped before a word starting with a vowel or h: <span class=la>multum ille</span> → <span class=la>mult' ille</span>.</p>
<h4>Caesura</h4><p>A word break inside a foot, usually in the 3rd foot – a natural pause.</p>
<h4>Elegiac couplet</h4><p>A hexameter + a "pentameter" (– ∪ ∪ – ∪ ∪ – ‖ – ∪ ∪ – ∪ ∪ –). Used for love poetry and epigram (Ovid, Catullus, Propertius, Martial).</p>
<p>Also met: <b>hendecasyllables</b> (Catullus), <b>iambic</b> meters (drama, Phaedrus), lyric stanzas (Horace's Alcaic, Sapphic).</p>`,
q:[['A dactyl is…',['– ∪ ∪','– –','∪ –'],0,''],['How many feet in a hexameter?',['5','6'],1,''],['Elision drops…',['a final vowel before an initial vowel','a consonant cluster'],0,'']]},
{u:6,t:'Roman names, dates, abbreviations & dictionaries',b:`<h4>Names</h4><p>Men: <b>praenōmen</b> (Gaius) + <b>nōmen</b> (family clan, Iūlius) + <b>cognōmen</b> (branch, Caesar). Women usually the feminine of the nōmen (Iūlia, Tullia).</p>
<h4>Dates</h4><p>Days counted <i>back</i>, inclusively, from three fixed points: <b>Kalendae</b> (1st), <b>Nōnae</b> (5th, or 7th in March, May, July, October), <b>Īdūs</b> (13th, or 15th in those months). <span class=la>Īdibus Mārtiīs</span> = 15 March; <span class=la>a.d. III Kal. Ian.</span> = 30 December. Years: <span class=la>a.u.c.</span> (from the founding of Rome, 753 BC) or named by consuls.</p>
<h4>Abbreviations</h4><p>M. Marcus · C. Gaius · Cn. Gnaeus · L. Lucius · P. Publius · Q. Quintus · Ti. Tiberius · S.P.Q.R. Senātus Populusque Rōmānus · S.D. salūtem dīcit (letters) · S.V.B.E.E.V. sī valēs, bene est, ego valeō.</p>
<h4>Using a dictionary</h4><p>Find the dictionary form: for nouns the nominative (strip the ending, test the genitive); for verbs the 1st sg. present. Recognise the 3rd/4th principal parts (e.g. <span class=la>tulit → ferō</span>, <span class=la>sublātus → tollō</span>). Recommended: Whitaker's Words (online parser), Lewis & Short / Oxford Latin Dictionary, Logeion (combines several dictionaries).</p>
<h4>Next steps</h4><p>Read daily. Start with adapted texts, then Caesar, Catullus, Ovid, Cicero, Vergil, Livy. Use the Reader tab and the Plan for structure.</p>`,
q:[['The Ides of March =',['15 March','13 March','1 March'],0,''],['In "Gaius Iūlius Caesar", the nōmen is…',['Gaius','Iūlius','Caesar'],1,''],['To look up "tulit" you search…',['ferō','tulō'],0,'']]}
];
